  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How dimensional weight works</h2>
    <p>Carriers charge for the space a package takes up in a truck or plane, not just its weight. They calculate a "dimensional weight" by multiplying length × width × height in inches and dividing by a number called the divisor. You pay for whichever is greater: the actual weight or the dimensional weight, rounded up to the next pound.</p>
    <p>At a divisor of 139, the one UPS and FedEx use for daily rates, a 16 × 12 × 10 in box has a dimensional weight of 1,920 ÷ 139 = 13.8, billed as 14 lb, even if the contents weigh 5 lb. Trim the box to 16 × 12 × 8 in and the dimensional weight drops to 11.05, billed as 12 lb. 2 inches of height saved 2 billable pounds on every shipment.</p>

    <h2>Ways to pay for less air</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Right-size the box.</strong> Cut down a box that's too tall, or stock 2 or 3 sizes that fit your most common items. The <a href="../../calculators/dimensional-weight.html">dimensional weight calculator</a> shows the exact cut that drops a billed pound.</li>
      <li><strong>Use mailers for soft goods.</strong> Clothing and other compressible items often ship in a poly mailer with no meaningful dimensional weight.</li>
      <li><strong>Round dimensions honestly.</strong> Carriers round each side up to the next whole inch before multiplying, so a 12.1-inch side counts as 13. Measure the box after it's packed, including bulges.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare rate types.</strong> Retail counter rates and online or commercial rates can use different divisors and prices. The same box can bill fewer pounds at one and cost less at another.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>When dimensional weight doesn't apply</h2>
    <p>Dense items, such as books, tools, or parts, usually weigh more than their dimensional weight, so actual weight sets the price. Some services also exempt small packages under a size threshold or use flat-rate pricing where any box that closes ships at one price. Check whether your typical package is weight-bound or size-bound before spending effort on box sizes: the calculator shows both numbers side by side.</p>
    <p>Carriers update divisors, thresholds, and surcharges periodically, and large or irregular packages can trigger additional handling fees beyond the billed weight. Confirm current rules with your carrier before changing packaging across your whole catalog.</p>
  </div>
</section>
